WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.053 --> 00:00:22.237
When Monsieur Gillenormand found the cards and locket belonging to Marius, he was furious.

2
00:00:22.998 --> 00:00:26.020
He sat in silence for an hour until Marius returned home.

3
00:00:26.961 --> 00:00:31.404
Then, as soon as his grandson opened the door, he lashed out at the young man.

4
00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:34.193
"So you're a baron now?"

5
00:00:34.753 --> 00:00:37.894
Monsieur Gillenormand held up one of Marius' calling cards.

6
00:00:37.914 --> 00:00:39.975
"What does this mean?"

7
00:00:39.995 --> 00:00:44.297
"It means that I am my father's son," replied Marius.

8
00:00:44.317 --> 00:00:47.038
"Your father's son?"

9
00:00:47.498 --> 00:00:49.599
Monsieur Gillenormand said harshly.

10
00:00:49.619 --> 00:00:52.180
"But I am your father."

11
00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:57.462
"My father was a humble and heroic man, who gloriously served France.

12
00:00:58.282 --> 00:01:04.873
He died forgotten and abandoned, and he had just one fault: He loved his country and me too

13
00:01:05.054 --> 00:01:08.796
much." This was more than Gillenormand could listen to.

14
00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:10.857
He sprang to his feet.

15
00:01:10.877 --> 00:01:13.378
"Marius, you abominable child!

16
00:01:14.619 --> 00:01:17.721
I don't know what your father was, and I don't want to know!

17
00:01:18.621 --> 00:01:23.504
What I do know is that the men fighting for Napoleon were all beggars, assassins, and thieves!

18
00:01:24.044 --> 00:01:25.545
They were cowards and traitors!"

19
00:01:26.545 --> 00:01:28.807
Marius shuddered at his grandfather's words.

20
00:01:29.427 --> 00:01:33.514
He had to defend his father, yet that would mean insulting his grandfather.

21
00:01:33.994 --> 00:01:34.955
How could he do that?

22
00:01:34.975 --> 00:01:37.637
"Down with King Louis XVIII!"

23
00:01:37.998 --> 00:01:39.039
he finally shouted.

24
00:01:39.959 --> 00:01:44.443
The king had been dead for four years, but it was the best insult that Marius could come

25
00:01:44.523 --> 00:01:45.424
up with at the moment.

26
00:01:46.365 --> 00:01:48.567
The old man turned whiter than his hair.

27
00:01:48.587 --> 00:01:51.730
"We cannot remain under the same roof.

28
00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:53.011
Be off!"

29
00:01:54.192 --> 00:01:58.976
Marius went away without saying where he was going, without knowing where he was going.

30
00:01:59.988 --> 00:02:05.272
He could find neither the locket nor his father's "will," so he assumed that his grandfather had hidden

31
00:02:05.312 --> 00:02:07.714
them somewhere or thrown them into the fire.

32
00:02:09.075 --> 00:02:14.019
Marius left with thirty francs, his watch, and a few things in a carpetbag.

33
00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.863
He hired a cab, jumped in, and headed toward the Latin Quarter.

34
00:02:19.784 --> 00:02:24.828
When Marius arrived outside the Cafe Musain, he recognized a fellow law student.

35
00:02:25.568 --> 00:02:29.672
Although he did not know this student very well, Marius got out of the cab.

36
00:02:30.208 --> 00:02:34.352
His new friend, Courfeyrac, knew of a place where Marius could stay.

37
00:02:35.353 --> 00:02:37.735
It was in the lodging house where Courfeyrac lived.

38
00:02:38.755 --> 00:02:41.898
Courfeyrac also introduced Marius to a group of friends.

39
00:02:42.859 --> 00:02:48.324
They belonged to a secret society of students and workingmen who wanted to improve the lot of the

40
00:02:48.364 --> 00:02:49.064
common man.

41
00:02:49.865 --> 00:02:52.887
Marius started to attend their meetings at the Cafe Musain.

42
00:02:53.428 --> 00:02:57.912
He began to feel so comfortable with these young men that he joined in their debates.

43
00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:01.895
One afternoon he loudly defended Napoleon.

44
00:03:03.196 --> 00:03:05.337
His remarks were greeted with silence.

45
00:03:06.538 --> 00:03:11.322
It was obvious that he was a bigger supporter of the former emperor than the other students.

46
00:03:12.283 --> 00:03:15.966
They wanted to push for a republic with a leader elected by the people.

47
00:03:16.506 --> 00:03:21.190
So Marius now felt isolated from his new friends as well as his grandfather.

48
00:03:21.971 --> 00:03:23.812
But Courfeyrac remained his friend.

49
00:03:24.333 --> 00:03:27.936
When the landlord demanded the rent, Courfeyrac tried to help Marius.

50
00:03:28.884 --> 00:03:29.924
"What are you going to do?"

51
00:03:30.404 --> 00:03:31.245
asked Courfeyrac.

52
00:03:31.845 --> 00:03:33.025
"How much money do you have?"

53
00:03:33.045 --> 00:03:36.126
"I have no idea," said Marius.

54
00:03:36.146 --> 00:03:38.207
"I only have 15 francs."

55
00:03:39.567 --> 00:03:41.128
"I could lend you the rent money."

56
00:03:41.148 --> 00:03:44.268
"No, I don't want to be in debt to anyone."

57
00:03:45.169 --> 00:03:50.510
Marius didn't mention that his grandfather had asked his aunt to send him six hundred francs every six

58
00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:56.332
months. Marius had returned the money to her, saying that he had enough to live on.

59
00:03:57.516 --> 00:04:02.160
"I know a clothes dealer who'll buy your overcoat and a pair of trousers," said Courfeyrac.

60
00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:03.801
"Do you have a watch?"

61
00:04:03.821 --> 00:04:05.803
"Yes, a gold one.

62
00:04:06.303 --> 00:04:11.487
If I sell the clothing, that will leave me with one pair of trousers, one waistcoat, a hat,

63
00:04:11.527 --> 00:04:12.047
and a coat.

64
00:04:12.608 --> 00:04:13.288
That'll be enough."

65
00:04:14.289 --> 00:04:17.692
"I also know a watchmaker who'll buy your watch," said Courfeyrac.

66
00:04:17.712 --> 00:04:20.474
"But what will you do after that?

67
00:04:20.854 --> 00:04:22.215
Can you read English or German?"

68
00:04:22.235 --> 00:04:29.199
"No." "That's too bad because a friend of mine, a publisher, needs someone who can translate articles into

69
00:04:29.239 --> 00:04:33.341
French. It doesn't pay well, but you can live on it."

70
00:04:33.361 --> 00:04:36.643
"Then I will learn English and German."

71
00:04:38.064 --> 00:04:41.506
Marius handed his overcoat, trousers, and watch to Courfeyrac.

72
00:04:42.406 --> 00:04:44.147
Life became hard for Marius.

73
00:04:44.748 --> 00:04:47.669
He moved to other lodgings, where he swept his own room.

74
00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:52.092
Sometimes he bought only a penny's worth of cheese at the market.

75
00:04:52.808 --> 00:04:56.570
He waited until evening to buy bread when the baker reduced the price.

76
00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:02.112
When he bought a lamb cutlet, he cooked it himself and made it last three days.

77
00:05:03.213 --> 00:05:08.195
Through all this, Marius managed to study law and to be admitted to the bar.

78
00:05:09.155 --> 00:05:10.276
Three years passed.

79
00:05:11.076 --> 00:05:17.319
By means of hard work, courage, and perseverance, Marius managed to earn seven hundred francs a year.

80
00:05:18.119 --> 00:05:23.618
He had learned English and German, and thanks to Courfeyrac, he had a job at the publishing house

81
00:05:23.798 --> 00:05:24.658
as a translator.

82
00:05:25.599 --> 00:05:28.260
Marius had survived a world of deprivation.

83
00:05:28.841 --> 00:05:33.863
In his struggle against poverty, he had done everything except go into debt.

84
00:05:35.104 --> 00:05:36.865
He didn't owe money to anyone.

85
00:05:37.826 --> 00:05:42.208
In all his trials he felt encouraged by a secret force within himself.

86
00:05:43.209 --> 00:05:50.012
Beside his father's name, another name was engraved upon Marius' heart: Thenardier.

87
00:05:50.032 --> 00:05:52.973
Marius had surrounded this man with a kind of halo.

88
00:05:53.834 --> 00:06:00.416
Thenardier was a hero, as far as Marius was concerned, because the innkeeper had saved his father's life.

89
00:06:01.477 --> 00:06:04.438
Marius had even gone to Montfermeil in search of Thenardier.

90
00:06:04.958 --> 00:06:09.260
There he had learned of the unlucky innkeeper's bankruptcy and ruin.

91
00:06:10.361 --> 00:06:14.322
Since then Marius had made many efforts to track the man down.

92
00:06:15.303 --> 00:06:21.113
For three years he had devoted himself to this search, spending what little money he could spare.

93
00:06:21.873 --> 00:06:24.574
But nobody could give him any news of Thenardier.

94
00:06:25.174 --> 00:06:27.014
It was thought that he had gone abroad.

95
00:06:27.895 --> 00:06:33.436
Even Thenardier's creditors could not find him, and they had been looking for him with even more zeal

96
00:06:33.496 --> 00:06:34.216
than Marius.

97
00:06:35.897 --> 00:06:42.098
Marius' father had left him only one debt to pay: to thank Thenardier and help the innkeeper in

98
00:06:42.259 --> 00:06:43.119
any way he could.

99
00:06:43.899 --> 00:06:47.260
And Marius had made it a point of honor to pay this debt.

100
00:06:47.956 --> 00:06:53.881
His only goal in life was to meet Thenardier one day and say, "You do not know me,

101
00:06:54.401 --> 00:06:57.144
but I know you and what you have done for my father.

102
00:06:57.784 --> 00:06:58.505
Here I am.

103
00:06:59.205 --> 00:07:00.126
Let me help you."

104
00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:05.090
As for Marius' relationship with his grandfather, things remained the same.

105
00:07:06.051 --> 00:07:09.213
Neither side attempted a reconciliation or sought a meeting.

106
00:07:09.794 --> 00:07:14.637
Marius imagined that his grandfather had never loved him, but he was mistaken.

107
00:07:15.598 --> 00:07:17.400
Gillenormand adored Marius.

108
00:07:18.216 --> 00:07:23.759
He had ordered everyone to never speak of Marius again, but he regretted that he was so well

109
00:07:23.879 --> 00:07:27.541
obeyed. At first he was sure that Marius would return.

110
00:07:28.102 --> 00:07:33.224
But weeks, months, and years passed without Marius arriving home.

111
00:07:34.585 --> 00:07:39.728
Old men need affection as they do sunshine, and Gillenormand missed Marius.

112
00:07:40.588 --> 00:07:46.752
While Gillenormand was suffering, Marius was congratulating himself on never thinking about the old man.

113
00:07:47.864 --> 00:07:50.266
Suffering had taken away Marius' bitterness.

114
00:07:50.826 --> 00:07:54.849
If he thought about his grandfather at all, it was only with kindness.

115
00:07:55.670 --> 00:08:00.994
But he had determined to take nothing more from the man who had been so cruel to his

116
00:08:01.054 --> 00:08:05.557
father. When his grandfather drove him away, Marius was a child.

117
00:08:06.077 --> 00:08:09.760
But poverty and suffering had made Marius a man.
